This program explores the girth and diameter of Ramanujan graphs.
The bipartite graphs have q^3-q vertices, and the non-bipartite
graphs have half that number. Each vertex has degree p+1.
Both p and q should be odd prime numbers;
  or you can try p = 2 with q = 17 or 43.

Choose a branching factor, p: OK, now choose the cube root of graph size, q: The graph has 336 vertices, each of degree 4, and it is bipartite.
Any such graph must have diameter >= 5 and girth <= 10;
theoretical considerations tell us that this one's diameter is <= 12,
and its girth is >= 8.
Starting at any given vertex, there are
       4 vertices at distance 1,
      12 vertices at distance 2,
      36 vertices at distance 3,
     102 vertices at distance 4,
     128 vertices at distance 5,
      53 vertices at distance 6,
       0 vertices at distance 7.
So the diameter is 6, and the girth is 8.

